<h2>Before you start</h2><p>Decide which channels you will support, who owns each ticket type, what agents need to know, and how managers will measure response and resolution. Menus and available features depend on product and plan, so confirm details in the <a href="https://support.zendesk.com/hc/en-us">official Zendesk Help Center</a>.</p>
<h2>1. Choose the product and plan</h2><p>Compare the current <a href="https://www.zendesk.com/pricing/">Zendesk plan table</a>. Support and Suite do not have identical scope. Confirm whether you need email ticketing, knowledge content, messaging, live chat, voice or AI capabilities before inviting the full team.</p>
<h2>2. Map the support workflow</h2><p>Write down ticket categories, priority rules, escalation owners and service targets before adding automations. Keep the first version small, define queues that match real work, and agree on how unassigned tickets will be reviewed.</p>
<h2>3. Connect customer channels</h2><p>Enable only channels your team can staff. Zendesk lists email and ticketing on Support Team, while Suite plans add broader capabilities. Verify plan dependencies in the official documentation, then submit a test request through each enabled channel.</p>
<h2>4. Set ownership and routing</h2><p>Define who receives each ticket type and how unassigned work is reviewed. Add rules for clear cases first, such as product area or customer priority. Keep an exception path for requests that do not match a rule.</p>
<h2>5. Create saved replies and internal notes</h2><p>Prepare concise templates for common questions, including the next action and when to escalate. Keep internal notes separate from customer-facing messages and review templates when product instructions or policies change.</p>
<h2>6. Build self-service content</h2><p>If your plan includes the required knowledge features, create clear articles for common issues. Each article should answer one question, list steps in order and explain when to contact support.</p>
<h2>7. Review reporting</h2><p>Use available dashboards to review volume, response times, resolution patterns and queues that accumulate work. Do not rely on one metric alone. A fast first response can coexist with a slow resolution.</p>
<h2>8. Plan exports and migration</h2><p>Zendesk documents JSON, CSV or XML exports, but availability can depend on plan and account settings. Its migration guide says ticket imports use API resources and some ticket metrics are not imported. Read the <a href="https://support.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/4408886165402-Exporting-ticket-user-or-organization-data-from-your-account">export guide</a> and <a href="https://support.zendesk.com/hc/en-us/articles/6696005837082-How-can-I-migrate-information-from-another-platform-into-Zendesk">migration guide</a>.</p>
<h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Enabling channels without staffing and ownership.</li><li>Creating complex automations before the ticket taxonomy is stable.</li><li>Assuming exports are enabled by default.</li><li>Moving records without checking which metrics and triggers transfer.</li><li>Choosing a plan without confirming billing and feature dependencies.</li></ul>
